Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.370
Hello and welcome back to
another video with ItsDrDan.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:07.510
And today we're going to be learning
about the chemistry behind electrolytes,

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.459
specifically those found within solutions,
which as many of you know, you see

00:00:12.489 --> 00:00:16.219
advertisements all over the place in
terms of Gatorade commercials, Powerade,

00:00:16.489 --> 00:00:20.290
all these different types of things that
we can ingest for going to the gym about

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.920
how you need electrolytes to replenish
your body and to help you move forward.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.340
Well, really, what exactly are they?

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.240
Well, when it comes to electrolytes, the
whole idea is that they are different

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.879
salts that we have in our body.

00:00:32.879 --> 00:00:38.029
So if you think of salts, like table salt,
they have at home, sodium chloride, right?

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.419
Where you have sodium and you also
have chlorine ions all bound together.

00:00:42.779 --> 00:00:47.000
And one thing about that is, well,
if you ingest salt, well, is it

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:48.860
going to still exist as a crystal?

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:52.940
Well, if you were to throw salt
in water, what happens to it?

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:56.579
So last time you made rice or
pasta at home, did it sit there

00:00:56.579 --> 00:00:58.310
and stay in its crystalline form?

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.770
And one thing you can imagine is
all the little water molecules.

00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:07.325
all around in that glass of water are
going to start surrounding those ions.

00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:11.245
They're attracted because waters,
as you can imagine, have a partial

00:01:11.245 --> 00:01:13.205
positive, partial negative side.

00:01:13.715 --> 00:01:16.515
They're going to be attracted
to all these little ions.

00:01:17.175 --> 00:01:22.505
Now, when you get enough of them, they
work together through ion dipole forces.

00:01:22.885 --> 00:01:26.525
To break these molecules apart
or break these compounds apart

00:01:27.005 --> 00:01:31.455
into their individual ions, so
they'll surround and conquer them.

00:01:32.004 --> 00:01:35.525
So when it comes to different ionic
compounds, we're always interested

00:01:35.525 --> 00:01:40.055
in trying to figure out exactly
how are they dissociating, meaning,

00:01:40.215 --> 00:01:41.805
how are they breaking apart?

00:01:41.875 --> 00:01:43.985
One thing turns into two.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:49.060
So breaking apart, so these electrolytes
is really the thing that's going to be the

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:54.239
focus of this video, especially when we're
looking in solutions and just a little

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.280
bit of a reminder, what are they solutions
or anytime you have a homogenous mixture.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:01.639
So one example of that is.

00:02:02.015 --> 00:02:05.325
If you look around you, right, the air
that you can't really see, that is a

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:11.675
homogeneous mixture being that the solvent
is nitrogen and your solute is oxygen.

00:02:12.184 --> 00:02:14.204
So the solute is the minor component.

00:02:14.325 --> 00:02:16.244
The solvent is the major component.

00:02:16.774 --> 00:02:19.785
Another example is if you go
back to salt and water above.

00:02:20.175 --> 00:02:20.535
Right.

00:02:20.535 --> 00:02:24.345
The salt is the solute
that we had in solution.

00:02:24.855 --> 00:02:30.665
And this aqueous tells you, Hey, that
you have water as your solvent and

00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:35.775
is dissolving all these ions that are
in solution to help them break apart.

00:02:36.434 --> 00:02:39.655
So let's take a look at what these
electrolytes are all about, how we

00:02:39.655 --> 00:02:44.015
can label them and learn the different
types of them and how you can identify

00:02:44.015 --> 00:02:45.605
them in your chemistry course.

00:02:51.555 --> 00:02:56.025
So starting off electrolytes are
substances whose aqueous solution.

00:02:56.415 --> 00:03:00.965
is a conductor of electricity,
meaning that these ions, which are

00:03:00.965 --> 00:03:04.055
positive, negative attract charge.

00:03:04.314 --> 00:03:07.795
So whenever you have charges,
well, you can actually

00:03:07.795 --> 00:03:09.085
conduct electricity with them.

00:03:09.545 --> 00:03:11.824
This is what your, these different
commercials, when you think of

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:15.864
Gatorades and professional athletes,
all showing how they are being used

00:03:15.915 --> 00:03:17.105
all the time to help fuel them.

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:19.599
It's to help make them better, right?

00:03:19.629 --> 00:03:20.909
And to function better.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.159
Electrolytes are something that's
essential for your body to function.

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:28.040
There are many different types of them
because what they do is inside of your

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:32.090
actual bloodstream and around your
cells, they cause different chemical

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.450
electro and electrostatic gradients
within the cells to help cause different

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.440
ions to flow, causing ATP to be
generated, which helps your body move.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.430
And that's very, very important.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.170
So to label them, the first
type, there are three overall.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.340
The first one is strong electrolytes.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.280
So when you think of strong, think
of really, really, really muscular.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:58.000
The way that we label these is
that these are all ionic compounds.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.840
Meaning that they will dissolve
and they will dissociate into ions.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:07.110
So what we, the way we label
ionic compounds is all of them are

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.100
referred to as strong electrolytes.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.510
And that's because ionic
compounds will dissolve in

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:15.560
water due to ion dipole forces.

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:19.960
So whenever we are labeling these,
the way to remember is all ionic,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:26.030
strong acids, strong bases, are all
considered to be strong electrolytes.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.600
Because they all can be
broken apart by water.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.060
And you might be like, wait a minute, Dr.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.970
Dan, what about solubility rules?

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.820
Aren't some of them
considered to be insoluble?

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:38.210
And that's true, some
of them are insoluble.

00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:42.739
But, even though they are labeled
as insoluble, the very, very, very,

00:04:42.739 --> 00:04:46.950
very tiny percentage of them do
break apart into ions, and they're

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:48.640
still labeled as strong electrolytes.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.380
We might not show them in a chemical
equation, but you still could

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.879
label it as a strong electrolyte.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:58.470
So these are a little bit easier than
having to work with solubility tables

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.030
and all those other different methods.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.069
Let's look at the next type.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:05.760
Weak electrolytes.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:11.689
So weak electrolytes are whenever a very
small percentage of the molecules in

00:05:11.689 --> 00:05:15.220
water, they are dissolved by the solvent.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:18.100
However, they don't all dissociate.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.570
They don't all break
apart into their ions.

00:05:20.924 --> 00:05:24.265
In fact, a very small
percentage of them do.

00:05:24.745 --> 00:05:28.695
So all of them are soluble, they're
all polar, but some of them will,

00:05:28.805 --> 00:05:33.085
most of them will stay as their actual
molecular form or as covalent molecules.

00:05:33.835 --> 00:05:38.564
So when these exist, you'll have
very few ions, we're talking like 0.

00:05:39.104 --> 00:05:42.895
1 to 5 percent of them
will actually dissociate.

00:05:43.365 --> 00:05:47.875
Now a way that we can label weak
electrolytes for easy context, for this

00:05:47.875 --> 00:05:49.975
course, and for introductory chemistry.

00:05:50.635 --> 00:05:56.905
is that all the weak acids and weak bases
are considered to be weak electrolytes.

00:05:57.425 --> 00:06:02.204
So, how do you exactly know when it's
a weak, a weak acid versus a weak base?

00:06:02.845 --> 00:06:06.695
Well, for you guys, there is a
list of strong acids and bases that

00:06:06.695 --> 00:06:08.534
you can find on the green sheet.

00:06:08.965 --> 00:06:11.825
And if you're someone that's watching
this video that's not in my course,

00:06:12.265 --> 00:06:14.785
then there's a video on this sheet.

00:06:14.795 --> 00:06:15.845
It's called the green sheet.

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.405
It's our solubility video.

00:06:17.924 --> 00:06:19.555
Take a look at it so
you can find out more.

00:06:20.275 --> 00:06:24.254
So if you are actually looking at
looking at this thing, there is a

00:06:24.254 --> 00:06:26.915
list of strong acids and strong bases.

00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:29.735
Find it for yourself so that
you can take a look at it.

00:06:30.015 --> 00:06:32.895
In fact, it's something
that you have seen before.

00:06:33.535 --> 00:06:38.015
Now with all that list, all of
those ones are strong electrolytes.

00:06:38.489 --> 00:06:43.070
So if it's not on that list as
a weak acid or base, that is

00:06:43.099 --> 00:06:44.710
going to be a weak electrolyte.

00:06:44.719 --> 00:06:47.819
So if it's not on the list,
that is a weak electrolyte.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.640
And as a hint to remember, okay,
well, what's an acid, what's a base?

00:06:52.049 --> 00:06:56.669
If it has an H in the front of
the formula, then it's an acid.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:01.170
If there's an OH in the back,
then it's considered to be a base.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:05.220
So remember that order when we're trying
to look at those different pieces,

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.040
so we can keep them all straight.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.810
The last one is non electrolytes.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:14.830
Now, non, or in this case,
whenever we have none of them,

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:17.140
are actually turning into ions.

00:07:17.799 --> 00:07:20.620
So you'll have all these
dissolved solutes, which means

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.680
you have polar molecules.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:26.570
Interacting through dipole dipole,
hydrogen bonding, you remember

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:28.590
the phrase like dissolves like.

00:07:29.109 --> 00:07:33.199
Well, they dissolve, however,
they do not dissociate, so they

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:35.389
don't become cations and anions.

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:38.020
They stick together as covalent molecules.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.669
They're a little too strong
to actually break apart.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:44.030
So when that happens, what kind
of molecules are these like?

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:47.310
Well, for example, one of them
could be like a sugar molecule here.

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:49.914
If you have sugar, this
thing does not dissolve.

00:07:50.195 --> 00:07:51.345
break apart into ions.

00:07:51.475 --> 00:07:53.285
But you know that sugar dissolves, right?

00:07:53.285 --> 00:07:56.195
You put in your tea, your
coffee, different fruit juices.

00:07:56.625 --> 00:07:59.364
You know that it dissolves in all of them.

00:07:59.395 --> 00:08:01.215
It's something that's in
a lot of our beverages.

00:08:01.895 --> 00:08:03.964
So, how exactly does that look?

00:08:03.995 --> 00:08:09.775
Well, if we were to write that, right,
if I had C6H12O6, well, it's a solid.

00:08:10.304 --> 00:08:14.814
But once you throw it into water,
it's going to become aqueous, right?

00:08:14.825 --> 00:08:16.265
So it's going to dissolve.

00:08:16.965 --> 00:08:20.965
Now, being that it's still, notice
how it's still in its molecular form,

00:08:20.965 --> 00:08:23.635
there's no plus or minus written.

00:08:24.134 --> 00:08:27.794
This is referred to as a non
electrolyte because it still dissolves

00:08:27.824 --> 00:08:32.755
being polar, but it's not, it's
not able to conduct electricity.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:33.270
Right?

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:34.770
So it's not an actual electrolyte.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:38.980
You can, you can ingest it, but it's not
going to give you those extra benefits

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:43.010
that are going to get absorbed and help
the different cells out in your body.

